.TH OOB-USB-SERIAL 1 "2026-11-04" "oob-usb-serial 2.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
oob-usb-serial \- out-of-band USB serial console multiplexer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B oob-usb-serial
.I command
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B oob-usb-serial
discovers every USB-to-serial adapter attached to the host and opens each one
as a named window inside a single detached
.BR tmux (1)
session, each window running
.BR tio (1)
on a stable
.I /dev/serial/by-id
(or, for adapters that share a serial number,
.IR /dev/serial/by-path )
link. Adapters are matched by their stable udev
.B ID_SERIAL
identifier, optionally narrowed to a USB port, rather than by their
.I /dev/ttyUSBx
node, which is not stable across reboots or replugs.
.PP
It is intended to turn a small Linux host, such as a Raspberry Pi Zero with a
USB hub and several serial adapters, into an out-of-band access box reachable
over SSH.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B discover
List discovered adapters as raw tab-separated records
(device node, ID_SERIAL, vendor, model, by-id path, by-path path).
.TP
.B list
Human-friendly table of adapters, their resolved configuration and the path
tio opens.
.TP
.B start
Start the detached tmux session, one window per adapter running tio.
.TP
.B attach
Attach to the session, starting it first if it is not running.
.TP
.B stop
Terminate the session.
.TP
.B restart
Stop the session (if running) and start it again. Use it to pick up adapters
plugged in after the start, or configuration changes.
.TP
.B status
Report whether the session is running (exit status 0) or not (exit status 3).
.TP
.B config
Map each discovered adapter into
.IR devices.conf ,
prompting for a window name and serial line settings; invalid answers are
asked again and adapters already mapped are skipped. Adapters that share an
ID_SERIAL with another attached adapter also get a
.B path
key. Writing usually requires root.
.TP
.B help
Show usage information.
.PP
The session commands
.RB ( start ,
.BR stop ,
.BR restart ,
.BR attach ,
.BR status )
always act on the session of
.BR OOB_USER .
Run as root, they switch to that user first; run as any other user, they
refuse.
.SH "TMUX KEYS"
Within the session:
.B Ctrl-b n
/
.B Ctrl-b p
switch consoles,
.B Ctrl-b w
lists them,
.B Ctrl-b d
detaches, and
.B Ctrl-b R
relaunches a console that has closed. Inside a console,
.B Ctrl-t q
quits tio.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/oob-usb-serial/oob-usb-serial.conf
Global defaults: baud, data, parity, stop, flow, session name, log directory
and
.B OOB_USER
(the account that owns the session).
.TP
.I /etc/oob-usb-serial/devices.conf
Per-device mappings in INI format. Each
.RI [ section ]
names a tmux window and contains a
.B match
glob (against udev ID_SERIAL) and/or a
.B path
glob (against the /dev/serial/by-path link), plus optional
.BR baud ,
.BR data ,
.BR parity ,
.B stop
and
.B flow
keys.
.TP
.I /run/oob-usb-serial/
Generated tmux configuration.
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/oob-usb-serial
Bash tab-completion for the subcommands.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR tio (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR udevadm (8)
.PP
Full documentation: https://github.com/diaznet/oob-usb-serial/wiki
